## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's practical understanding of per-source ordering with shared throughput, backpressure mechanisms, and graceful degradation in a realistic chat notification system.

## Key Concepts

- **Per-room FIFO buffers**: Ensure events within each room maintain insertion order
- **Global bounded worker queue**: Limit total memory usage while enabling cross-room parallelism
- **Backpressure thresholds**: Prevent producers from overwhelming the system
- **Degradation via event coalescing**: Reduce load by merging rapid state changes (typing indicators) into single events
- **Graceful shutdown**: Drain in-flight work before terminating without data loss

## Code Example

```javascript
class RoomBuffer {
  constructor(maxBacklog = 1000) {
    this.queues = new Map();
    this.maxBacklog = maxBacklog;
  }

  enqueue(room, event) {
    if (!this.queues.has(room)) {
      this.queues.set(room, []);
    }
    const queue = this.queues.get(room);
    if (queue.length >= this.maxBacklog) {
      if (event.type === 'typing') {
        return this.coalesceTyping(room, event);
      }
      return false; // backpressure signal
    }
    queue.push({ ...event, seq: this.getNextSeq(room) });
    return true;
  }

  coalesceTyping(room, event) {
    const queue = this.queues.get(room);
    const last = queue[queue.length - 1];
    if (last?.type === 'typing') {
      last.count = (last.count || 1) + 1;
      return true;
    }
    queue.push({ ...event, count: 1 });
    return true;
  }
}
```

This implementation provides per-room ordering via sequence numbers, backpressure via bounded queue depth, and automatic coalescing of typing events during high load.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a bounded, low-latency queue with per-partition ordering, backpressure, and graceful shutdown. It also probes fault-tolerance concepts like spill-to-disk for handling overflow scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded ring buffer with atomic indices for lock-free operations
- Per-partition ordering guarantees using sequence numbers
- Lock-free or low-lock synchronization with proper memory ordering
- Backpressure strategies to prevent resource exhaustion
- Graceful shutdown coordination with worker notification
- Spill-to-disk path for overflow handling

## Code Example
```go
// Simplified data structures (illustrative)
type Entry struct {
  PartitionID int
  Sequence   uint64
  Data       []byte
}

type RingBuffer struct {
  Buffer    []Entry
  Capacity   int64
  Head       int64  // Consumer position
  Tail       int64  // Producer position
  Mask       int64  // Capacity-1 for modulo operations
  Full       bool
  Shutdown    int32  // Atomic shutdown flag
}

func (rb *RingBuffer) Enqueue(entry Entry) error {
  for {
    head := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Head)
    tail := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Head)

    if (tail-head) >= rb.Capacity {
      // Buffer full, apply backpressure or spill to disk
      return rb.spillToDisk(entry)
    }

    // Attempt to advance tail using CAS
    newTail := (tail + 1) & rb.Mask
    if atomic.CompareAndSwapInt64(&rb.Tail, tail, newTail) {
      rb.Buffer[tail] = entry
      return nil
    }
  }
}

func (rb *RingBuffer) Dequeue() (Entry, bool) {
  for {
    if atomic.LoadInt32(&rb.Shutdown) == 1 {
      return Entry{}, false
    }

    head := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Head)
    tail := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Tail)

    if head == tail {
      return Entry{}, false  // Empty
    }

    // Attempt to advance head using CAS
    newHead := (head + 1) & rb.Mask
    if atomic.CompareAndSwapInt64(&rb.Head, head, newHead) {
      return rb.Buffer[head], true
    }
  }
}
```

